From: WinBoat
Subject: [PATCH] viogpu3d: blob resources and host-visible memory for Venus

Vulkan in the guest (Mesa's Venus driver) keeps its command rings and all
host-visible device memory in blob resources that the host maps into the
device's host-visible memory window (virtio shared memory region 1, a 64-bit
BAR). The driver did not know about blobs, context types or that window.

- Acknowledge VIRTIO_GPU_F_CONTEXT_INIT, and VIRTIO_GPU_F_RESOURCE_BLOB when
  the device has a host-visible memory window (found by walking the virtio
  vendor capabilities for a shared memory capability with id 1).
- RESOURCE_CREATE_BLOB, RESOURCE_MAP_BLOB and RESOURCE_UNMAP_BLOB on the
  control queue, and fences on a context's ring (VIRTIO_GPU_FLAG_INFO_RING_IDX).
  A new blob is attached to its context (CTX_ATTACH_RESOURCE): Venus looks
  the blobs its commands name up in the context, and Linux' driver attaches
  them the same way.
- Four escapes for the user-mode Vulkan driver, all per D3DKMT device (its
  virgl context): VIOGPU_BLOB_CREATE, VIOGPU_BLOB_MAP (maps the blob at a
  free range of the window, then into the calling process with the caching
  the host asks for), VIOGPU_BLOB_DESTROY and VIOGPU_EXECBUFFER (submits a
  command stream on a ring and sets an event when its fence signals; the
  stream may be empty, which Venus uses just to have a ring's fence signal).
- Blobs a process leaves behind are unmapped and destroyed with its device.
- The adapter info reports SupportsBlob, so the user-mode driver can tell.

The window is handed out by a first-fit allocator; dxgkrnl's memory manager
is not involved, since these allocations never back a D3D resource.
---
---
